

Thank you for the update. The November 4 deadline is tight, so here is the short version first:
•	Dataset: ready, reused from the 2025 and 2026 editions.
•	Baseline: our existing starting kit, plus a quantization path we will add.
•	Evaluation: unchanged, except that PSNR is measured on the quantized model.


A little more on each below. This is the intended direction rather than the final rule set, and the details are still ours to settle together.
1. Proposed topic: Quantized Burst HDR and Restoration
The task, the dataset and the primary metric stay exactly as in the previous two editions. Only one axis changes: the submitted model must be a quantized integer model, and PSNR is measured on that quantized model.
This continues the arc of the series:
•	2025: what quality is reachable at all? Budget 30 M parameters / 4.0 T FLOPs. Winner 43.22 dB.
•	2026: how much survives a mobile budget? Budget 5 M parameters / 100 G FLOPs. Winner 41.556 dB.
•	2027: how much survives integer deployment? Budget roughly 0.2 M weights, a small INT8 artifact.
•	
2. Dataset: ready
We reuse the synthetic burst dataset from the 2025 and 2026 editions: 9 RAW Bayer frames per scene, three exposure levels, 768 × 1536, degraded with noise, translation, rotation and motion blur, with frame 0 aligned to the ground truth.
300 train / 20 val / 20 test scenes are already hosted and can be released on day one. No new data collection is required.

3. Baseline: our starting kit, plus a quantization track
Our starting kit from the previous two editions (github.com/Eve-ctr/RawFusion) already provides the data loader, the training loop and the U-Net reference model. We reuse it as is.

What we add for 2027 is a quantization reference path: PTQ export to QDQ ONNX, a QAT recipe, baseline PSNR reported in both FP32 and INT8, and a checker that lets a team validate its own submission offline before uploading.
None of this code has to exist for the proposal. We can write it once the challenge is confirmed.
4. Evaluation
The primary metric is PSNR of the quantized model on the 20-scene test set, with SSIM secondary, as before.

The submission flow stays the one participants already know:
•	Validation phase, about 3 weeks: participants submit output images, scored by CodaLab on a live leaderboard, exactly as in 2025 and 2026.
•	Test phase, about 1 week: participants also submit the quantized ONNX model, the code and the factsheet. We then re-run the submitted model, and that score is the official one.
For ranking I would keep PSNR as the single ranked axis and express everything else as hard caps, rather than as a weighted score. A formula only creates a rule surface for teams to optimise against.
For the efficiency side, my current thinking is a published rule rather than a measurement, so that a team can check its own submission offline before uploading. Very roughly: a size limit of a few MB on the quantized model file, replacing the parameter cap; INT8 weights and activations, with INT16 allowed where needed; and a pinned ONNX opset with an operator allowlist, replacing the FLOPs cap.
These are a sketch of the shape rather than a proposal of the numbers. The actual limits, and whether an allowlist is even the right instrument, are exactly what I would like us to work out together.



Please let me know if the direction works for you. If there is anything further you would like to discuss, just send me an email and I will get back to you as quickly as I can.



 

